      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) are associated with some common critical cardio-metabolic risk factors. The aim of this study was to explore the association between intrahepatic triglyceride (IHTG) content and CKD in obese subjects."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "A total of 1068 obese participants received anthropometric, biochemical measurements and hepatic ultrasonography. Of those, 485 participants received magnetic resonance spectroscopy ((1)H-MRS) for the determination of IHTG content. CKD was defined as a urinary albumin:creatinine ratio (UACR)≥30 mg/g and/or estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR)<60 mL/min per 1.73 m(2)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "The prevalence of CKD was significantly higher in NAFLD subjects compared to subjects without NAFLD, while the prevalence of CKD was gradually increased as the IHTG content increased by quartiles (P for trend<0.001). After adjustment for multivariate metabolic factors, the risk of abnormal albuminuria and CKD was increased by 68% [OR (95% CI): 1.68 (1.21-2.33), P<0.01] and 54% [OR (95% CI): 1.54 (1.14-2.07), P<0.01] respectively per one standard deviation (SD) increase in IHTG content. The association between IHTG content and CKD was not changed by conventional risk factors, including age, BMI and hypertension (all P<0.05)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "IHTG content is independently associated with CKD in obese adults."
          }
        ]
      },
